✅  RIP : In RIP (Routing Information Protocol), the metric is used to determine the best path to a destination network. The metric used in RIP is "Hop Count".

🔹 Hop Count represents the number of routers a packet must pass through to reach its destination.
🔹 The fewer hops, the better the route.
🔹 If multiple paths exist, RIP selects the route with the lowest hop count.
🔹 The maximum hop count in RIP is 15. If a destination requires 16 hops or more, it is considered unreachable.

✅  EIGRP : Enhanced Interior Grateway Protocal ) is a distance vector protocal
 🔹 



✅  OSPF : 
In OSPF (Open Shortest Path First), the metric used to determine the best route is called "Cost".
🔹 OSPF Cost = Reference Bandwidth / Interface Bandwidth
🔹 Lower cost = Better path
🔹 By default, the reference bandwidth is 100 Mbps

✅  IS-IS :
🔹 The default metric in IS-IS is "10" per interface.
🔹 Unlike OSPF, IS-IS does not automatically consider bandwidth in its metric calculation.
🔹 Metrics can be manually assigned to interfaces to influence path selection.


✅✅ Administrative Distance: 
🔹 Administrative Distance (AD) is a value (0-255) used by routers to select the most trustworthy route when multiple routes to the same destination exist from different routing protocols.

Lower AD = Higher Trust
Higher AD = Lower Trust
AD helps routers prioritize routing protocols when multiple paths exist

🔹 Connected Interface - 0 -   Most Trusted
   Static Route    1  - High
   EIGRP (Internal)    90 - Medium
   OSPF    110 - Medium
   IS-IS   115 - Medium
   RIP 120 - Low
   EIGRP (External)    170 - Lower
   Unknown Route   255 - Untrusted (Never used)

